Start();
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields Utilities list;

debug Start;
register(Utilities);
define
    codeBuffer buffer;
    openBrace="{";
    closeBrace="}";
    /***************************************************************************
        genParseTest walks one rule and generates a code attribute w/parse code for
        the rule.
        Note: getParseTest assumes it just works. But what if it dont?
        Note: the use of actionTypE gate in walkRules to avoid rerunning genParseTest
        if has already been run. The test is at the top of walkRules so you have to
        get into walkRules for the gate to happen.
        Note: we may want to add debugging print messages at top of genParseTest
        and compile, at least on our initial runs on a single rule.
        BTW the no attributes no members case is handled by the first setParse
        call in genParseTest. Look that over with monocle in place and a squinty eye.
    ***************************************************************************/
    /*  ⚠ PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION FOR THE POST-MINT RE-RUN, written down
        BEFORE it runs so it cannot be fitted afterwards.
        The 2026-08-17 census measured prune-noPrint at 17, and all seventeen
        were the ONE shared CodE node. After the mint fix gives every rule its
        own CodE -- properly noPrint, because processCode sets that on what it
        attaches -- every member walk that meets a CodE should prune it.
            PREDICTED prune-noPrint = 34
            derived as generated(54) minus empty-condition bodies(20), since a
            rule with no terms never enters the member loop at all.
        READING IT: ~34 confirms per-rule flag hygiene. Still 17 means the mint
        did not change what the walk sees. 54 would mean zero-length rules are
        entering the loop too, which would say `length` is being read after the
        attach rather than before.
        This is the SECOND independent certification of the mint -- bodyT proves
        distinct bodies by POINTER, this proves flag hygiene by CENSUS. Two
        mechanisms, one fix.  */
    genParseTest argument code={
        reset(codeBuffer);
        printTO(codeBuffer);
        print openBrace:;
        setParse(argument);
        if datA != 0;
            if listLengtH; print "rules should not have data and a list; one or the other":;
            cerr "CENSUS leaf-install :" taG:;
            printTO(0);
            return;
        if hasAttributeS;   conjunct = "AND";
        else                conjunct = "OR";
        print `"if ";
        iterate grup on argument;
        while ++grup;
            setParse(grup);
            print $taG "()";
            if nexT;    print _conjunct;
            else        print ";";
        @argument;
        print $"return runRuleAction(this);":;
        print `closeBrace:;
        clear(CodE);
        CodE = codeBuffer;
        codeCopy <- copyOf(CodE);
        codeCopy :. noPrinT;
        argument +% codeCopy;
        argument :. isCodeD;
        printTO(0);
        @CodE;
        print taG "=" CodE:;
        };
    walkRules argument code={
        if actionTypE == 0;
            length  = listLengtH;
            if datA != 0;   print ~$taG "=" argument;
            else            print ~$taG;
            genParseTest(argument);
            compile(argument);
            if length > 0;
                iterate grup on argument;
                while ++grup;
                    if noPrinT;     continue;
                    if binTypE;     continue;
                    if isRule;      walkRules(grup);
        else    cerr taG "already has code so no parse generated":;
        };
    /*  ⚠ THE DRIVER IS AN ACTION, AND ITS `stop()` IS INSIDE IT. That is not
        style -- it is what makes this file finish. Measured 2026-08-19.

        Once the walk installs generated methods, THE LOADER CAN NO LONGER READ
        THE SOURCE THAT FOLLOWS. A top-level `walkRules(X); stop();` therefore
        never reaches its `stop()`: the parser goes back for the next statement,
        finds it cannot read one, and dies with `checkInput: no input source`.
        An action body is parsed ONCE, at define time, into a cached BlocK, so
        everything in here is already-parsed code and needs no loader. Calling
        `stop()` from in here ends the process before the loader is ever asked
        for another statement.

        ⚠ AND THE OLD SHAPE'S EXIT STATUS WAS AN ACCIDENT OF WHAT FOLLOWED IT.
        With five dead `walkRules` lines sitting below the `stop()`, the run
        exited 0 -- the poisoned loader failed to parse them and abandoned the
        file, which is the documented exit-0-on-parse-failure path. Truncate
        those same lines and the identical run exits 139. NEITHER run reached
        `stop()`, and neither said so. That is why the sentinel below is on
        `cerr`: stdout is block-buffered and a crash loses it, so a stdout
        sentinel cannot report the crash it exists to detect.

        MEASURED TARGET MATRIX, this shape, 2026-08-19 binary -- all five reach
        the sentinel, print `stop:`, and exit 0:
            TokenXP 59 lines · Braced 59 · NumbeR 19 · QuotE 7 · StringXP 7
        `Start` still exits 1 with no sentinel, and it is NOT this file's bug:
        it stops at the first `ERROR processCode: define parse failed` because
        `compile` exits the process on a refused parse. That is fixIts F-17e,
        and it is yours.

        ONE TARGET PER RUN. The `stop()` ends the process, and a second walk
        could not read its own driver line anyway. Swap the name below; the
        others are parked here rather than left under the `stop()` as lines
        that look live:
            runParse(QuotE);  runParse(NumbeR);  runParse(StringXP);
            runParse(TokenXP);  runParse(Braced);                            */
    runParse argument code={
        walkRules(argument);
        cerr "PARSER SENTINEL -- walk complete":;
        stop();
        };
    ;

runParse(TokenXP);
